WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:08.830
MORGAN MCLEAN: Hi.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.000
Welcome to "Cloud OnAir"
live from Seattle.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.820
We host webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:13.900
My name's Morgan McLean.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.762
I'm the product manager for
our Stackdriver APM products.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:18.220
And today I'm going
to tell you how

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:21.130
to make your apps fast and
reliable with Stackdriver APM.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.750
You can ask questions anytime.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.840
And we'll do a Q&amp;A
session at the end.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.220
So as I mentioned, we're talking
about Stackdriver APM today.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.740
It's the tools that
we offer that'll

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.680
allow the next wave
of service developers

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.960
to manage their apps as
reliably and performantly

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.540
as we do at Google.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.710
Stackdriver APM is composed
of three products, Stackdriver

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:42.247
Profiler, Stackdriver Debugger,
and Stackdriver Trace.

00:00:42.247 --> 00:00:44.080
I'll go and do an
overview of these products

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.140
in a few minutes, and
then we'll do a live demo.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.090
As I mentioned before,
these are the tools

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:51.020
that unlock the same
methodologies that we've

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.230
honed at Google for
years to our customers

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:54.770
and to the broader market.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.220
As you may know, Google's
been an extremely large

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:01.090
microservices operator
for almost a decade now,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.839
and we've developed a lot of
our own methodologies, tools,

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:06.130
and techniques that we now
want to share with our cloud

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.770
customers to make
them more successful.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:11.890
Stackdriver APM can be used on
apps anywhere, not just those

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.490
running on GCP.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:17.167
So you've probably heard of
the Stackdriver brand before.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.000
It's been around for
some time, particularly

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.640
with our monitoring product.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.320
Stackdriver is
the suite of tools

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.830
that we offer to customers
to improve their development

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.502
experience on the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:27.502 --> 00:01:31.230
Stackdriver Monitoring is what's
most well-known generally.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.834
It was acquired a few
years ago from a startup.

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:35.250
And it's been
brought into Google.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.770
And it has stats and
dashboards and alerts.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.020
We also have Stackdriver
Logging and Error Reporting,

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.166
which does logs ingestion and
analysis for our customers,

00:01:43.166 --> 00:01:45.790
and can also highlight logs that
appear to be errors, and alert

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.830
you on those.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.840
What I'm talking about
is Stackdriver APM.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.840
So this is our suite of
developer-focused tools.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.360
It includes distributed
tracing, production debugging,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.490
and continuous
profiling, all of which

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.710
we use at Google every day.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:00.554
So over the past
few years, Google's

00:02:00.554 --> 00:02:02.970
been on a bit of a journey as
a part of its Cloud Platform

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.410
initiative.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.810
We've made a lot of headway
and made a lot of waves

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.360
by providing parts of
Google infrastructure

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.440
to our customers.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.930
Google had to invent a
number of new methodologies,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.930
whether this was high-scale
container orchestration

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.670
or new storage
technologies, in order

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.080
to operate at extreme scale.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.782
And we've exposed
these to customers.

00:02:20.782 --> 00:02:22.740
Some of these are through
open source projects,

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.990
like Kubernetes and
Istio, where we've

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.360
exposed our internal platform--

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.490
Borg is Kubernetes
and Stubby is Istio--

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:33.450
but others are exposing services
through the Cloud Platform.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.980
Examples of this include
Spanner, GKE, and BigQuery.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.744
And there's many, many others.

00:02:39.744 --> 00:02:41.910
The next sort of pillar of
what we've been providing

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.430
is allowing customers to
emulate our practices.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.370
So the most visible
example of this

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:53.122
is the "Site Reliability
Engineering" bible, which

00:02:53.122 --> 00:02:54.330
we published a few years ago.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.538
And there's a second revision
in the works right now.

00:02:56.538 --> 00:02:58.740
I think it's being
published pretty soon.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:02.370
And this allows customers
to use the same SRE

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.440
practices and
methodologies that we've

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.090
used across our
SRE and dev teams

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.841
at Google to keep
ourselves at scale.

00:03:08.841 --> 00:03:11.730
Finally, Stackdriver
represents the tools

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.440
that we offer to customers.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.940
And so this is the
third pillar that

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.850
would allow companies to emulate
a lot of the infrastructure

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.850
practices and tools that we have
at Google to operate incredibly

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.170
successfully.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.130
So for the past 10,
20 years, developers

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:27.690
have been on a journey.

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:30.180
If you think back
10 or 15 years ago,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.970
as a developer, if you
started at a new company,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.755
it wouldn't be
entirely uncommon,

00:03:34.755 --> 00:03:36.630
particularly at smaller
shops, if they didn't

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:40.280
have some form of source control
or bug tracking or code review.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.870
And if you go back a
slightly less amount of time,

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:45.690
maybe five or 10 years ago,
for a services company,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.700
again, it wouldn't
be entirely uncommon

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.340
if they didn't have some form of
distributed logging or metrics

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.150
or alerts.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.540
It's just those tools were new,
they were sometimes expensive,

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.316
and not everywhere
had adopted them yet.

00:03:56.316 --> 00:03:57.690
But today, as a
developer, if you

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.220
went to a firm of
almost any size

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.928
and they didn't have
those, your instinct

00:04:00.928 --> 00:04:03.095
would probably be to
walk right out the door,

00:04:03.095 --> 00:04:04.470
even if it was on
your first day,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.090
because everybody
in the industry

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.070
understands that things
like source control,

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:11.650
bug tracking, metrics, alerting
is incredibly important.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:14.380
And it's difficult,
if not impossible,

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:17.310
to become a successful
company without these tools

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.870
and these methodologies.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:21.670
We believe that distributed
tracing, production profiling,

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.670
and production debugging
are on the cusp

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.620
of being this next wave.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.920
With microservices,
in particular,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.020
distributed tracing is
such a powerful concept,

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.720
and so are the others,
that in a few years,

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.930
our hypothesis is that it
will be very, very difficult

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:37.990
to operate without those.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.990
And indeed, if you look
at companies like Google

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.790
or others who
operate at similarly

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.530
high-scale and
reliability, you'll

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.540
see that tools like
tracing, in particular,

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:46.660
are very, very common.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:51.630
And so it's our belief that
this is going to trickle down

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:54.430
to all companies of all sizes.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.880
So let's dig into the APM suite.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.161
The first component
is Stackdriver Trace.

00:04:59.161 --> 00:05:01.410
This is probably one that
at least some of the viewers

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.490
will be more familiar with.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.350
Distributed tracing
over the last year or so

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.620
has been making
a lot of inroads.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.039
Stackdriver Trace is a classic
distributed tracing solution.

00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:11.580
And what that means
is that it allows

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.890
you to view real customer
requests as they come

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.750
into your services,
and you can view them

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.897
as they propagate across
different services.

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:20.230
So if a request comes into
your front end from service A

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:23.630
and propagates to services
B, C, D, E, and so on,

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:25.770
a distributed trace
captures that.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.660
It's incredibly useful for
understanding service topology.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.870
So understanding how those
services call each other.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.570
And indeed, in an incredibly
complex microservices

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.550
deployment, sometimes
it might be the only way

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.170
that you know that.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.390
Secondly, traces are incredibly
powerful for latency analysis.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:44.185
So we use distributed tracing
at Google almost every day

00:05:44.185 --> 00:05:46.025
to understand how we
can make Google.com

00:05:46.025 --> 00:05:50.005
and our other services
faster and more reliable.

00:05:50.005 --> 00:05:52.380
The power of a distributed
trace is that not only does it

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:54.450
show you the hops
that a request takes,

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.620
but it also shows you how
long it takes at each hop,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.700
and also which points
were parallelized versus

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.620
where you had a bottleneck.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:03.450
And so it's very,
very powerful when

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.970
you want to reduce the latency
of a service, particularly

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.590
tail latency.

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.172
Google has made a
lot of investments

00:06:09.172 --> 00:06:10.380
in this space over the years.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.610
And so you may have heard
of something called Dapper.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.430
Dapper was a distributed
tracing solution

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.400
that we created at
Google a long time ago.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.710
We published a white paper
on it, I believe, in 2010.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.237
And in fact, if you look at
many other distributed tracing

00:06:22.237 --> 00:06:24.570
solutions that are available
on the market, many of them

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.240
will reference that white paper
as being what sort of gave them

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.770
the idea for building this.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.570
And we're exposing
Dapper to our customers

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.640
through Stackdriver Trace.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.260
The next is
Stackdriver Profiling.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.370
This is actually the newest
entrant to our APM suite.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:40.440
Stackdriver Profiler is
a continuous statistical

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.167
profiler.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.750
So it's meant to be
run in production,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:45.030
much like our other
tools, continually.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:47.862
It's not a profiler that you
turn on at certain points.

00:06:47.862 --> 00:06:50.070
And it's not a profiler that
hinders your performance

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.410
at all.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:53.996
Continuous profiling
is very powerful.

00:06:53.996 --> 00:06:56.370
It allows you to understand
the call patterns of your app

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.951
in production, like,
the actual function call

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:01.200
patterns within your code
as opposed to a trace, which

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:04.000
shows you, generally,
service call patterns.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.350
And it's very powerful for
understanding how much CPU

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.245
and memory every function uses.

00:07:09.245 --> 00:07:11.615
If anyone watching is a
Microsoft developer, which

00:07:11.615 --> 00:07:14.490
I imagine many of
you are, you might

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:16.897
understand that profiling,
much like debugging,

00:07:16.897 --> 00:07:18.730
which we're going to
talk about in a minute,

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.330
is generally something that you
do for client app development.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:22.121
It's very powerful.

00:07:22.121 --> 00:07:23.430
It's very useful.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.800
But when you've moved to
web services, particularly

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.670
microservices, it's
very difficult.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:29.760
With Stackdriver Profiler,
we give you this data

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.320
and allow you to discover
where time's being spent,

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.090
where memory is being spent.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.160
And so it's very powerful
for general performance

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:40.500
improvements as well
as reducing your costs.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.620
And we use the same technology
behind this at Google every day

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.920
across almost all of
our biggest services.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.350
Finally, we have Stackdriver
Debugger, which in some ways

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:52.470
is probably the most magical
of these three tools.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:56.130
Stackdriver Debugger is
a production debugger

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.040
that's safe for use in
production environments

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.560
with real customer traffic
going through them.

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.530
Rather than creating
traditional breakpoints,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:04.990
it creates what
we call snapshots,

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.020
which are effectively
breakpoints except they

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:08.280
don't halt execution.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.490
So you can lay them down in our
very simple IDE-like interface

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.647
that shows your code.

00:08:12.647 --> 00:08:14.730
When they get triggered
with real customer traffic

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:16.104
or if you trigger
them yourselves

00:08:16.104 --> 00:08:18.870
by mocking a
request, they capture

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:20.730
the local variables
and the call stack,

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.469
but they don't halt
execution, which

00:08:22.469 --> 00:08:24.510
makes them really, really
powerful for root cause

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:25.680
analysis.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:28.500
Debugger also has another
feature called Logpoints, which

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:31.590
allows you to add log
statements to your application

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.996
across all of its
instances in real time,

00:08:33.996 --> 00:08:35.370
so you don't have
to redeploy it.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.226
So again, really powerful
root cause analysis tools.

00:08:39.226 --> 00:08:41.309
And let's just double-click
on these for a second.

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:46.360
So all of these services
offer lightweight open source

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:47.170
instrumentation.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:50.185
So for tracing and
for app-level metrics,

00:08:50.185 --> 00:08:52.060
our instrumentation is
done through a project

00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:54.070
that we run called
OpenCensus that we're

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.200
collaborating on with a
number of other vendors.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:57.790
And OpenCensus
provides libraries

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.165
that you link to
your app that'll

00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:01.302
capture traces,
app-level metrics,

00:09:01.302 --> 00:09:03.010
and you can also add
your own information

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.810
to get sent back to us.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:07.287
Our remaining products,
so Profiler and Debugger,

00:09:07.287 --> 00:09:08.870
also have open source
instrumentation.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:10.536
It's not part of
OpenCensus, but they're

00:09:10.536 --> 00:09:12.090
all open source on GitHub.

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:14.534
And we think this is really
powerful for developers,

00:09:14.534 --> 00:09:16.450
because they can understand
how we're actually

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:17.740
interacting with their app.

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.323
If, for some reason,
they need to make

00:09:19.323 --> 00:09:22.710
a change to the instrumentation,
they can do so and rebuild it.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.817
And it's really easy
to use, because it's

00:09:25.817 --> 00:09:27.400
tied in to your
source control system.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.840
So you're not deploying agents
to a VM or updating them there.

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:34.537
You're simply, typically,
linking a library

00:09:34.537 --> 00:09:35.370
to your application.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.140
Or for Java, you're referencing
it again as a Java agent.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.280
You can rebuild your app, check
in that change, and deploy it.

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.490
And you can roll forward
and back between versions

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:45.210
through source control.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:46.959
So it's really, really
powerful and really

00:09:46.959 --> 00:09:49.396
easy to use for developers.

00:09:49.396 --> 00:09:50.770
We support a number
of languages.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:54.840
So on Trace and Debugger, we
support Java, Go, Node.js,

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.920
.NET, Ruby, Python, and PHP.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.760
On Trace, we also support
C++ instrumentation.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:00.700
Profiler is quite new.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.650
So the amount of
instrumentation is lower.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:06.279
Right now, we have support
for Java, Go, and Node.js.

00:10:06.279 --> 00:10:08.320
And we're building right
now, and hopefully it'll

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:12.410
ship pretty soon,
instrumentation for Python 3.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.920
Profiler is free during
the beta test period.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.810
Debugger is currently free.

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:20.690
And Trace is free up to 2 and
1/2 million spans per month,

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:21.610
which is quite a bit.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:25.340
We expect most customers to
stay within the free tier.

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:27.630
And so now I'm actually
going to hop to a live demo.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:35.250
So what I've done is I've taken
a microservices application

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:37.402
actually developed by
Weaveworks called Sock Shop.

00:10:37.402 --> 00:10:38.610
And it's available on GitHub.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:40.440
And it's a really
useful application

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.980
for testing out both monitoring
systems and microservices

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:44.730
deployments.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:46.230
And we've taken
this code, and we've

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:49.160
deployed it to a GKE instance.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:51.540
And so you can see all
of the services here.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.480
And Sock Shop's a really
great demo for our products

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:56.517
both because it has,
I think, roughly eight

00:10:56.517 --> 00:10:58.600
different services written
in different languages,

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:00.390
so it's got three Java
services, three Go

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.580
services, and, I think,
two Node.js services,

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:07.829
but it's also useful because
it wasn't written by us.

00:11:07.829 --> 00:11:09.870
When you're a vendor,
there's always an incentive

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.911
to build a demo that works
with your technologies

00:11:11.911 --> 00:11:13.330
and can show off
your golden path.

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:16.314
Sock Shop's really powerful
because it emulates a journey

00:11:16.314 --> 00:11:17.730
that many of our
customers will go

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:19.810
on when they're taking
their application,

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.380
whether it's running
on-prem or in GCP,

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.172
and trying to instrument
it in Stackdriver.

00:11:24.172 --> 00:11:26.463
And so it was a great learning
experience for the team,

00:11:26.463 --> 00:11:29.010
but also a great demonstration
for our customers for demos

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:32.220
like this to see how our tools
interact with a codebase that

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:33.780
might resemble their own.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.420
So let's dive into
Stackdriver Trace.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.850
This is the main UI
for Stackdriver Trace.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:42.180
We present captured
requests, or traces,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:44.190
on a scatterplot-style view.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:45.790
This is useful for two reasons.

00:11:45.790 --> 00:11:48.180
First off, you can get
a general understanding

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.080
of the latency profile
of your application

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.200
from the scatterplot.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.290
But secondly, it makes it
really easy to acquire traces.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.750
Today, our sampling for
traces-- so we don't trace every

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.680
request, we trace a certain
percentage of them--

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.790
our sampling is somewhat random.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.276
And so, generally,
as a developer,

00:12:05.276 --> 00:12:06.900
you're going to be
interested in traces

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.760
that have some kind of
unique characteristic,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.301
whether that's ones that have
high latency, which is what I'm

00:12:11.301 --> 00:12:13.207
clicking on in this
case, or traces

00:12:13.207 --> 00:12:15.540
that are correlated from error
or some other interesting

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:16.190
event.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:18.000
And a scatterplot
makes it really easy

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:21.000
to acquire, in particular,
the high latency traces.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.550
And so I'd run a load
test against Sock Shop,

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.781
and generated a very high
latency burst of traffic.

00:12:26.781 --> 00:12:28.280
In fact, the latency,
at some point,

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:29.920
was up to 40 milliseconds.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:33.180
And we can use this UI to
quickly acquire some traces.

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:35.760
Now, most of these are
requests to the cart service,

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:36.930
which are fairly simple.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:37.597
Request goes in.

00:12:37.597 --> 00:12:38.971
Something gets
added to the cart.

00:12:38.971 --> 00:12:39.870
Response goes back.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.328
But what we'll do
is we'll actually

00:12:41.328 --> 00:12:43.710
jump to a request made to
the orders end point, which

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:45.585
generates a really
powerful trace, because it

00:12:45.585 --> 00:12:48.930
crosses a number of services
and has some custom annotations.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:53.820
And as you can see here, we have
a much more verbose, much more

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.649
powerful distributed trace.

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:57.690
And so we can click through
this and see metadata

00:12:57.690 --> 00:12:58.620
from each component.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.512
These components
are called spans.

00:13:00.512 --> 00:13:02.970
You can understand which spans
call each other versus which

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.969
are parallel with the
parent-child relationships

00:13:04.969 --> 00:13:06.610
that we show here.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.190
But really importantly,
you can see

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.719
where this trace became
actually distributed rather

00:13:10.719 --> 00:13:12.010
than just being a simple trace.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:15.000
So you can see, while this trace
originated on the orders end

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:18.300
point, it also
hopped to addresses,

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:20.850
it also then hopped
to the cart service,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.540
and eventually went to
the shipping service.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:26.204
And so with a classic
tracing system,

00:13:26.204 --> 00:13:27.870
you wouldn't be able
to see any of this.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.328
But the distributed
trace allows us

00:13:29.328 --> 00:13:31.380
to both understand
the service topology

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.300
and also where the
latency was spent.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.520
And so you can imagine,
as a developer,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.370
if you were tasked with
speeding up this service,

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.790
you'd probably start looking
around here at the cart service

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:44.370
or further here down in
the shipping service.

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:45.780
Indeed, there's
a async call here

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:47.529
that doesn't appear
to be actually running

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:48.410
asynchronously.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:50.076
And so you might want
to start with that

00:13:50.076 --> 00:13:52.380
for your latency
analysis investigation.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:55.520
Next, I'm going to show
Stackdriver Debugger.

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.210
So as I mentioned,
Stackdriver Debugger

00:13:57.210 --> 00:13:59.784
is very similar to a
traditional debugging solution

00:13:59.784 --> 00:14:01.200
that you may have
used previously,

00:14:01.200 --> 00:14:03.990
whether that's, like, a
standard interactive debugger

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.300
in IntelliJ or Visual
Studio, except it's

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.587
running in production.

00:14:07.587 --> 00:14:09.670
And it's running across
all instances of your app.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.711
You don't have to attach
it to a single instance.

00:14:11.711 --> 00:14:14.280
You just open Debugger
and just start working.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.990
And I'm going to show you
some of the functionality.

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:18.540
So we have a function here.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:21.900
This is the URL handler for
adding a new item to the cart.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:24.455
We're in the front end service,
which is written in Node.js.

00:14:24.455 --> 00:14:26.580
Now, what I'm going to do
is just a simple example.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.070
I'm going to lay down a
new snapshot on this line,

00:14:29.070 --> 00:14:31.980
and we're going to look for
the value of customer ID.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:34.789
So if we go back to
Weaveworks, and we add an item

00:14:34.789 --> 00:14:36.330
to our-- or sorry,
not to Weaveworks,

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:40.440
to Sock Shop, and add an item to
our cart, you can see it there.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.260
And we jump to our cart.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.100
Then we can jump to Debug.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:46.440
And we can see that we've
now captured the snapshot.

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:49.035
Now, in a real production
app with real traffic,

00:14:49.035 --> 00:14:50.550
a snapshot without
many conditions

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:52.357
will get triggered
almost instantly.

00:14:52.357 --> 00:14:53.940
That's because you'd
be capturing data

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:55.170
from real customer requests.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:56.640
And if you have
concerns about PII,

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.190
we have ways of hiding that
from your developers as well.

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:02.340
And so in this example,
we can see that we've

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:03.810
captured my customer ID here.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.630
And if I wasn't signed
in to the Sock Shop demo,

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:09.510
you'd see a null customer ID.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.740
Another cool thing that
we can do is copy this.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:13.663
We can actually set
it as a condition.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:21.500
And we can reapply the
snapshot or apply another one

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:23.130
on the line above.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:24.800
And we can rerun the request.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:26.330
And while this
doesn't necessarily

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.610
generate an interesting
example, because, of course,

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:31.070
we know my customer
ID, it really

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:33.440
shows off how we have similar
tools, as you can see,

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.740
we captured-- similar tools
to traditional debuggers.

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.990
So we can use custom
conditions and we

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:42.200
can use expressions
to show the values

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:45.320
of certain specific variables
that you want to capture.

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.150
And we also show
the call stack here.

00:15:47.150 --> 00:15:48.920
So it's a really
powerful debugging tool,

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.350
you can imagine, for
root cause analysis.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:53.009
You can use this
to find out both

00:15:53.009 --> 00:15:55.550
of the state of your system and
if certain control flow state

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.010
systems aren't
working as you expect.

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:00.650
The next feature that I
want to show is Logpoints.

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:03.440
So Logpoints allows you
to add log statements

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:05.980
to your application instantly.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.990
And you can add conditions
or use your own log,

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:11.190
just add a custom state.

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:13.750
So what we'll put here is--

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:15.570
we're going to print
out the customer ID.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:21.110
So first, we're going
to say, OnAir Webinar.

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.840
And we'll print out
the customer ID.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:25.970
And we'll apply this.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:28.670
And one of the really nice
things about Logpoints

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:32.010
is that it works with
different logging systems.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.130
So in this case, we're
using Stackdriver Logging,

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.110
which, as I mentioned before,
is our in-house logging solution

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:38.810
that we offer to GCP customers.

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:41.060
But if you were using another
logging solution or just

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:43.940
capturing logs locally,
Stackdriver Debugger Logpoints

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:45.082
would work fine.

00:16:45.082 --> 00:16:47.010
And it's really, really nice.

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.807
So we'll add this to the cart.

00:16:48.807 --> 00:16:50.390
And that will capture
a log statement.

00:16:50.390 --> 00:16:53.840
And this can take-- this isn't
as immediate as a snapshot.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:56.850
But we start searching
through our logs here.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.710
Sorry.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.080
We're going to say logpoint.

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.734
And you can see, we captured
our log statement right here.

00:17:06.734 --> 00:17:08.609
And we can view it inside
of the Debugger UI.

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:12.252
So you don't even have to jump
between Debugger and Logging

00:17:12.252 --> 00:17:14.460
when you're performing these
kinds of investigations.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.980
It's really, really nice.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.136
Finally, we're jumping
to Stackdriver Profiler.

00:17:20.136 --> 00:17:22.010
Now, I admit this is a
different GCP project.

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:24.060
We have one that generates
really nice profiles

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:25.018
and is great for demos.

00:17:25.018 --> 00:17:26.460
So I've switched to that.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:30.820
And the code in this app
that we're profiling,

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.262
it's an application that
constantly zips and unzips

00:17:33.262 --> 00:17:33.780
a file.

00:17:33.780 --> 00:17:36.090
So it's basically just
compressing and decompressing

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.080
data continually.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.420
And so it generates a
really nice flame graph

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:43.130
as opposed to a demo app that
gets very little service.

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.480
What you can see here
is the flame graph

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.840
that we generate from
capturing those profiles.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:50.504
On the vertical
axis, you can see

00:17:50.504 --> 00:17:52.170
the different names
of functions and you

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:53.320
can see their hierarchy.

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:55.800
So in this case,
[INAUDIBLE] once,

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.900
which calls
writer.write, and so on.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.000
And on the horizontal axis,
you can see the amount

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.560
of the resource being consumed.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.160
In this case, we're
looking at CPU time.

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:07.560
But Profiler also supports heap
profiling on languages other

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:09.040
than Java-- we're
working on Java,

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:11.310
there's some restrictions
from the JVM--

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:13.704
as well as thread and
contention profiling.

00:18:13.704 --> 00:18:15.120
And as I mentioned
before, this is

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:17.580
a continuous
statistical profiler.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.287
So we have effectively
no performance impact

00:18:20.287 --> 00:18:21.120
on your application.

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:22.494
It runs always in
the background.

00:18:22.494 --> 00:18:24.840
So you can capture real data.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:27.570
But the way we do this is
by having the Profiler agent

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.850
generally run on a maximum
of one instance at a time,

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:32.610
and only take samples
every so often.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:35.250
So we'll capture profiling
data for an hour or a day

00:18:35.250 --> 00:18:37.050
or a week or longer,
and then we'll

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:39.991
take the aggregation of that
and build this flame graph.

00:18:39.991 --> 00:18:42.240
So it gives you a really
good view into general system

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:44.567
performance.

00:18:44.567 --> 00:18:46.650
So you can see in this
example on the flame graph,

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:49.016
if you were a developer who
had been tasked with either

00:18:49.016 --> 00:18:51.390
improving the performance of
this application or reducing

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:53.790
its cost, you'd probably
be interested in this

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.640
compressor.deflate function,
and that's because it takes up

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:59.640
a lot of horizontal area,
just like the ones above it,

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:00.670
but its children don't.

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.795
And so it has a very high,
what we call, self time.

00:19:02.795 --> 00:19:07.140
In this case, 53% of the CPU is
being consumed by this function

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:08.462
and not by its children.

00:19:08.462 --> 00:19:10.920
And as a developer, you'd be
very, very interested in that.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:12.441
Now, if you know
about compression,

00:19:12.441 --> 00:19:14.190
then you probably know
the deflate process

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:14.940
will take a while.

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:16.050
That might be expected.

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.390
But we've had customers
who've had examples

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:21.510
where this was a function they
didn't expect it to be, like,

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:23.790
a regular expression
or something else,

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:27.090
where a significant percentage,
not 50% or anything like that,

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.452
was consumed by code
that should not have

00:19:29.452 --> 00:19:30.660
been consuming that much CPU.

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:32.326
And as a developer,
you can very quickly

00:19:32.326 --> 00:19:33.810
see that from the flame graph.

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:36.240
We had a number of customers
in the alpha report

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.050
that with about roughly one
hour of work with Profiler,

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:42.480
they saved about 10%
off their bill, which

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:43.715
is incredibly powerful.

00:19:43.715 --> 00:19:46.090
And if you can imagine operating
at extremely high-scale,

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:49.230
like some of these groups,
it's a lot of money.

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:52.650
If you were tasked with
doing a micro optimization

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.870
or improving a small part
of the system performance,

00:19:55.870 --> 00:19:58.830
you can also just click on
your area, we'll zoom in,

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:00.600
and you can then
get a better view

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:05.010
of where your CPU time or memory
or other data is being spent.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:07.020
So really, really powerful.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:08.052
Profiler is quite new.

00:20:08.052 --> 00:20:09.510
We have some new
features that will

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:14.186
be coming out fairly shortly,
including what we're calling--

00:20:14.186 --> 00:20:15.810
we don't really have
a name for it yet,

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:18.960
but what we're calling sort of
weighted profiling, where we're

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:21.150
going to give a higher
weight to profiles

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:23.430
that are captured at periods
of high CPU or resource

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:24.210
consumption.

00:20:24.210 --> 00:20:25.810
So you can get a
better view of how

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:28.230
your system's performing in
the worst case as opposed

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.490
to the average case.

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:33.210
And so that makes it more
powerful as a solution

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:34.225
to detect tail latency.

00:20:34.225 --> 00:20:35.850
We also have some
integrations that are

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:38.430
pending with Stackdriver Trace.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.490
So let's hop back
to our presentation.

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:47.400
So to recap over what
we just went through,

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:50.430
because I know it was
quite a bit, we start,

00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:52.080
we have Stackdriver Debugger.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.480
Debugger snapshots and
dynamic log statements

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:58.050
remove a lot of steps from your
root cause analysis cycles.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:00.540
So you can imagine that
snapshots or laying down

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:03.177
logpoints would replace your
traditional debugging steps

00:21:03.177 --> 00:21:04.260
that you might have today.

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:06.780
At a lot of companies when
there's a code error, what they

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:08.779
have to do is, when they
don't know where it is,

00:21:08.779 --> 00:21:10.510
they add log statements
to their code.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:16.212
They then redeploy their app
with the error still in tow,

00:21:16.212 --> 00:21:17.670
because they don't
know where it is

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:19.170
and they don't
know how to fix it.

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:21.300
Once the app is redeployed and
they look at the [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:22.830
log statements,
hopefully it tells them

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.871
where the code error was
or what the problem was.

00:21:24.871 --> 00:21:26.520
But it might not
always, and then you

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:28.030
have to redo that
steps again and again.

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:29.696
And if you have a
slow deployment cycle,

00:21:29.696 --> 00:21:32.680
that takes a long time.

00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:35.450
And so Debugger reduces
this to almost zero.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:38.820
You can use snapshots to
find those issues to find out

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.370
if certain if statements or
loops are being triggered,

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:43.981
if things are not operating
the way you expect them to be,

00:21:43.981 --> 00:21:45.480
without having to
redeploy your app.

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:47.730
And you can you do it across
all of the instances of your app

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:48.280
seamlessly.

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:50.280
So no more attaching a
debugger, no more testing

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:51.840
in a non-production environment.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.610
So really, really powerful.

00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.350
We use this at
Google all the time.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.183
It's a pretty magical
feature that we wanted

00:21:57.183 --> 00:21:58.980
to expose to our customers.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.470
Next we have Profiler, which
operates at extreme scale

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.561
and isn't limited to
analyzing single transactions.

00:22:04.561 --> 00:22:06.810
One of the biggest uses that
we've seen with customers

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.055
is both cost reduction, but
also finding slow memory leaks.

00:22:10.055 --> 00:22:11.980
And because it operates
over a long time

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.980
and because you can
change those time filters,

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.599
by dragging your
view through time,

00:22:15.599 --> 00:22:17.640
you can actually see if
certain functions are now

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.431
consuming more and more
memory, which imply

00:22:19.431 --> 00:22:20.846
that there's a leak there.

00:22:20.846 --> 00:22:22.470
We have a number of
new features coming

00:22:22.470 --> 00:22:25.900
to Profiler throughout
the rest of the year.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:28.260
And we're really excited
to see where it goes.

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:30.451
Finally, we have
Stackdriver Trace.

00:22:30.451 --> 00:22:32.200
Distributed tracing
for the last few years

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.140
has become generally
better understood

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:36.910
by a lot of our customers and
indeed the industry at large.

00:22:36.910 --> 00:22:39.990
But tracing tools tend to be
either expensive or difficult

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:40.500
to adopt.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:42.840
There are some open source
solutions in this space.

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:45.630
Generally, deploying your
own can be quite challenging.

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:48.000
And a lot of the paid
solutions are quite costly.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.000
Stackdriver Trace isn't.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.930
It's easy to get started with.

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.490
For most customers,
it's effectively free.

00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:56.490
And it can give you deep insight
into how your service topology

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:02.310
works and also how to reduce
latency in your application.

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:04.900
I want to touch on
instrumentation one more time.

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:07.950
So all of our instrumentation
is Apache-licensed and open

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:10.200
source, and it's on GitHub.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.760
All of it-- or we generally--
not always the case

00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:14.760
with Debugger, because
it's not quite as useful.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.850
But we generally expose
APIs that developers can use

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:19.140
to capture your custom data.

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:21.090
Now, what I mean by
that is for tracing,

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.410
you can create your own spans.

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:23.970
You can add
annotations to spans.

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:26.640
So in the demo that I just
did, some of those spans

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.924
didn't necessarily
represent RPCs or requests

00:23:28.924 --> 00:23:30.090
that went to other services.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.430
They were, rather,
created by the developer

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:35.340
to track what they thought
were long-running processes.

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:37.750
And for Profiler,
we also, similarly,

00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:40.465
have APIs that you can use
to capture custom data.

00:23:40.465 --> 00:23:42.090
For Debugger, on some
of the languages,

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:47.090
we have APIs that allow you to
arbitrarily capture a snapshot.

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:50.015
Our traces and app-level metrics
are captured via a project

00:23:50.015 --> 00:23:52.140
that we call OpenCensus
that we're collaborating on

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:53.880
with other vendors.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.180
OpenCensus effectively
automatically captures traces,

00:23:57.180 --> 00:23:59.680
allows you to annotate them,
captures app-level metrics,

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:01.590
and then will send them to
the back end of your choice.

00:24:01.590 --> 00:24:03.339
And Stackdriver is one
of those back ends.

00:24:03.339 --> 00:24:06.180
But we also support Prometheus,
Zipkin, and a number of others.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:13.370
I mentioned that we had a alpha
test of Stackdriver Profiler.

00:24:13.370 --> 00:24:16.670
And so one of the groups that
joined the alpha test with us

00:24:16.670 --> 00:24:17.810
was Snapchat.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:21.830
And so they had a lot of
success with Profiler.

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:24.320
They were able to
find areas in the code

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:27.230
where they had been consuming
more CPU than they expected.

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:29.480
So it was performing more
slowly or costing more money

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:30.890
than they had desired.

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:34.130
And they used Profiler
to both highlight those,

00:24:34.130 --> 00:24:35.900
because they didn't
know about them,

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:38.400
and also to then optimize them.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:40.800
And so they were happy
enough that they provided us

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.880
with a nice quote that I'm
able to share with you here.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:46.240
But Snap was certainly
not the only participants

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:47.030
in this alpha.

00:24:47.030 --> 00:24:48.620
I know at Google,
at GCP, we like

00:24:48.620 --> 00:24:50.922
to talk about Snapchat
a lot in particular.

00:24:50.922 --> 00:24:52.130
We had a number of companies.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:54.860
And as I mentioned before,
we heard from a number

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:56.060
of customers that they--

00:24:56.060 --> 00:24:58.130
with roughly one hour
of time, and they

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:00.380
were able to shave about
10% off of their bill.

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:07.920
So let's sort of summarize
what we've talked about.

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:10.190
So Stackdriver APM is
an essential toolkit

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:12.510
for the next wave of developers.

00:25:12.510 --> 00:25:15.290
It unlocks the methodologies
and the tools and techniques

00:25:15.290 --> 00:25:17.510
that we've used at Google
over the past decade

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.790
to operate at extreme
scale and to ship codes

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:21.410
safely and quickly.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:24.350
And it provides these to
customers around the world.

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:26.040
These customers might
be running on GCP.

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.290
They might be running on-prem.

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:30.290
They might not even use some
of our services, and that's OK.

00:25:30.290 --> 00:25:31.910
We want them to use
Stackdriver APM.

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:34.326
We're really excited and really
proud of the functionality

00:25:34.326 --> 00:25:36.560
that it provides.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:41.780
Developers can use these tools
generally on web services,

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:45.020
but also on other things,
their slow-running processes.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.630
And it really is going to
just provide a lot of insight

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.040
and a lot of
confidence, I think,

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:51.510
to engineering organizations
about their ability

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:53.450
to understand what
their service is doing

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:56.360
and their ability to
ship code rapidly.

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:59.940
As I mentioned before, it works
on services hosted anywhere,

00:25:59.940 --> 00:26:03.610
whether that's any other
cloud or on-premises.

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:05.600
And we're pretty
excited about it.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:11.090
All right, so that wraps
up the presentation.

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:13.827
We're going to hop to
Q&amp;A in a few minutes.

00:26:13.827 --> 00:26:14.660
Thank you very much.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:03.081
All right, we're back!

00:29:03.081 --> 00:29:05.080
So we gathered some
questions from the audience.

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:06.690
And I'm going to answer these.

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:10.110
The first question is, can I
debug Node.js or Angular code

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:13.420
running on my local
machine, like my laptop?

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:16.390
How can different reports help
in understanding the issues?

00:29:16.390 --> 00:29:18.060
So I'm going to
guess that by debug,

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:19.590
you mean Stackdriver Debugger.

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:22.050
And, yes, you can
use it to debug code

00:29:22.050 --> 00:29:23.820
that's running locally
or anywhere else.

00:29:23.820 --> 00:29:26.490
We've used it both internally
at Google and other places

00:29:26.490 --> 00:29:28.230
where, in fact, the
instrumentation is

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:29.749
running on someone's laptop.

00:29:29.749 --> 00:29:31.290
One of the other
things that I forgot

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:33.330
to mention during
the demo is that you

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:35.280
can also-- to view
the source code,

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:37.830
you have to pull it in
from another source, sort

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:40.010
of syncing it from another
source to pull it in.

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:45.040
And so if your code is stored
on GitHub, on Bitbucket,

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:48.390
on GitHub Enterprise, on
GitLab, or the Google Cloud

00:29:48.390 --> 00:29:50.730
repositories, you can
just use your credentials

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:52.980
to automatically
make the code show up

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:54.510
in Stackdriver Debugger.

00:29:54.510 --> 00:29:57.120
The other thing you can do for
this laptop debugging scenario,

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:00.480
where you might not want to
be storing the code elsewhere

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.630
or because you're just doing
something simply locally is you

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:07.280
can-- and if we can
just cut to my screen--

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:11.590
in the Debugger, you can go
to [INAUDIBLE] source code UI,

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:13.440
and you can actually
select Local Files.

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:16.364
And what this allows you to do
is actually pull in source code

00:30:16.364 --> 00:30:18.030
that's stored locally
from your machine.

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:20.460
This is useful for that
local debugging scenario

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:21.750
that you're asking about.

00:30:21.750 --> 00:30:23.580
But this is also
useful when you've

00:30:23.580 --> 00:30:25.170
made edits that are
running elsewhere,

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:27.532
not on your primary
service, or when you know

00:30:27.532 --> 00:30:29.490
the exact version of code
that's being deployed

00:30:29.490 --> 00:30:30.779
and you want to debug it.

00:30:30.779 --> 00:30:32.820
So you're not forced to
pull it in through GitHub

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:34.257
or other sources.

00:30:34.257 --> 00:30:36.090
Another interesting
thing about this feature

00:30:36.090 --> 00:30:39.224
is that we actually don't
save your code to Google.

00:30:39.224 --> 00:30:40.890
It simply syncs locally
to your browser.

00:30:40.890 --> 00:30:41.800
Never gets sent to us.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:43.966
And so if you have any
security concerns or concerns

00:30:43.966 --> 00:30:46.630
about Google accessing your
code, you don't need to worry.

00:30:46.630 --> 00:30:48.630
It's all pulled in just
locally to your browser.

00:30:48.630 --> 00:30:50.730
Never gets transmitted.

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:53.100
The next question
is about Profiler.

00:30:53.100 --> 00:30:55.200
The question is, is
Python on the roadmap?

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:56.880
If so, which quarter?

00:30:56.880 --> 00:30:59.340
Same question for Scala.

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:01.860
So Python is actually
already being worked on.

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:04.320
Right now we're working
on Python 3 support.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:05.700
We plan on supporting Python 3.

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:07.290
We're debating
Python 2 right now.

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:10.662
If you have opinions
about supporting Python 2,

00:31:10.662 --> 00:31:12.870
please let us know on the
Cloud Insiders mailing list

00:31:12.870 --> 00:31:15.710
or through in-app feedback.

00:31:15.710 --> 00:31:17.790
But, yes, Python's
on the roadmap.

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:21.540
My understanding is it's going
to ship within the next two

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:22.952
months.

00:31:22.952 --> 00:31:23.910
Don't quote me on that.

00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:25.860
But that's my understanding.

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:30.000
And that will be Python
support for VMs and containers.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:32.970
So that includes the
Google Compute Engine,

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:35.640
Google Kubernetes Engine,
or VMs or containers

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:36.900
running elsewhere.

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:39.930
We're going to revisit the
support [INAUDIBLE] standard

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:40.570
coming up soon.

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:43.440
We know that Python's incredibly
popular there as well.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:45.540
Same question for Scala.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:47.604
I'm not certain about that.

00:31:47.604 --> 00:31:49.020
I'd say I have to
get back to you,

00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:50.811
but I don't actually
know how to reach you.

00:31:50.811 --> 00:31:52.770
But Scala, I don't know.

00:31:52.770 --> 00:31:54.840
I know that Debugger
supports Scala.

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:58.440
But I'm not certain if
Profiler does or does not.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:01.500
It ties into the JVM
via the agent interface.

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:03.744
So I suspect it might just work.

00:32:03.744 --> 00:32:05.160
But I can't speak
with confidence.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:07.320
I apologize.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:08.970
The next question.

00:32:08.970 --> 00:32:11.280
What's this OpenCensus thing
that I keep hearing about?

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:12.604
How's that related to this?

00:32:12.604 --> 00:32:13.770
How does OpenTracing relate?

00:32:13.770 --> 00:32:15.840
That's a very good question.

00:32:15.840 --> 00:32:18.840
So OpenCensus, as I mentioned
during the presentation,

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:21.760
is an instrumentation
project for tracing

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:24.030
and for app-level
metrics and tags

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:26.760
that we're participating on
with a number of other vendors.

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:29.490
OpenCensus actually
ties back to a project

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:31.080
at Google called Census.

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:32.907
And we probably could
have gotten clever

00:32:32.907 --> 00:32:33.990
with the open source name.

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:35.550
But whatever.

00:32:35.550 --> 00:32:38.730
And Census, it's a library
that's automatically

00:32:38.730 --> 00:32:43.020
attached to every job
running at Google,

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:44.670
so every container
running at Google,

00:32:44.670 --> 00:32:47.040
that automatically captures
traces and metrics,

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:49.054
and performs the
appropriate correlations.

00:32:49.054 --> 00:32:51.220
OpenCensus is just our
externalized version of this.

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:52.886
We've been really
successful internally.

00:32:52.886 --> 00:32:56.970
We want to bring those same
benefits to the community.

00:32:56.970 --> 00:32:59.897
And part of the reason that
we've been participating

00:32:59.897 --> 00:33:01.980
with other vendors is that
tracing instrumentation

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.290
can be very difficult.
One of the challenges

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:05.850
of distributed
tracing is that you

00:33:05.850 --> 00:33:08.340
want to be able to automatically
propagate contexts,

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:11.220
so like a trace ID, for
example, from incoming

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:13.770
to outgoing requests, and
do that across services.

00:33:13.770 --> 00:33:17.790
This is somewhat easier inside
of a company like Google,

00:33:17.790 --> 00:33:21.052
where we generally use the
same libraries and same APIs.

00:33:21.052 --> 00:33:23.010
But in the external world,
this is challenging,

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.290
because you need to
support every language

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:28.560
that your developers use, so
we target eight for OpenCensus,

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:31.600
as well as every single web
framework, RPC framework,

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:33.850
storage library, and so on.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:35.820
So you can generate
both interesting traces

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:37.950
or, at a minimum,
propagate context.

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:40.537
This becomes very difficult.
It's very expensive for vendors

00:33:40.537 --> 00:33:42.120
to do this, which
is why we kicked off

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:45.150
this effort with the community
and with other vendors

00:33:45.150 --> 00:33:47.310
to work together on
building these libraries.

00:33:47.310 --> 00:33:49.530
And to be clear, OpenCensus
is a set of libraries.

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.834
So each library contains
the instrumentation,

00:33:51.834 --> 00:33:53.250
so those hooks
into web framework,

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:54.374
storage clients, et cetera.

00:33:54.374 --> 00:33:58.710
It contains APIs that you
can use to manually interact

00:33:58.710 --> 00:34:01.170
with traces and metrics, and
then it contains exporters,

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:04.980
which will send these back to
either Stackdriver or SignalFx

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:07.840
or whatever back end
that you're using.

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:10.570
And so that's sort of leading
into this next question

00:34:10.570 --> 00:34:12.945
about OpenTracing, and that's
sort of the big difference.

00:34:12.945 --> 00:34:15.060
OpenTracing provides
a generalized API

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:17.550
for interacting with
traces specifically.

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:21.570
OpenCensus provides not just
APIs, but instrumentation

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:25.230
and an implementation that allow
developers to operate on these.

00:34:25.230 --> 00:34:27.330
The APIs for OpenTracing
and OpenCensus

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:29.100
are slightly different.

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:30.630
For tracing,
obviously, OpenCensus

00:34:30.630 --> 00:34:34.440
has many more APIs for metrics
and stats and other things.

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:35.969
They differ very slightly.

00:34:35.969 --> 00:34:39.780
We've had some meetings with the
OpenTracing group about maybe

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:42.054
sort of alleviating that.

00:34:42.054 --> 00:34:43.679
But at a minimum, I
think we've already

00:34:43.679 --> 00:34:45.510
taken a pull request
that adds sort

00:34:45.510 --> 00:34:47.489
of a supplemental
OpenTracing API

00:34:47.489 --> 00:34:50.261
to OpenCensus if you have any
OpenTracing instrumentation.

00:34:50.261 --> 00:34:52.469
So if you've already made
investments in OpenTracing,

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.199
that'll still work
on OpenCensus.

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:55.920
All right.

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:57.330
Next question.

00:34:57.330 --> 00:35:00.270
For Profiler, is there some
smart profiler concepts

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:03.570
that highlights likely
problem areas, a la ML,

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:06.270
for inspection for a developer
or a trained production support

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:06.870
engineer?

00:35:06.870 --> 00:35:09.330
That is another great question.

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:11.370
So Profiler, as I
mentioned, is quite new.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:14.490
While we've been using it for
some time inside of Google,

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:16.560
it's only just
reached beta state,

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:19.050
I think, two months ago
for external customers

00:35:19.050 --> 00:35:20.700
through Stackdriver Profiler.

00:35:20.700 --> 00:35:24.640
And so we're adding a ton
of features as we speak.

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:29.635
So if I think about the
concept of smart profiling,

00:35:29.635 --> 00:35:32.430
I talked about the weighted
profiling during the demo.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.430
And so what this
will do is allow you

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:39.030
to more selectively [INAUDIBLE]
your application when

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:41.980
it's running into CPU
or memory problems.

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.864
So you'll get a more effective
flame graph out of that

00:35:44.864 --> 00:35:46.780
that you can use to
perform your own analysis.

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:48.360
And that's really
useful when you're

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:51.450
investigating poor performance
as opposed to just general cost

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:52.470
reduction.

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:54.870
And so that's a sort of
smart profiler concept--

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:56.370
though it doesn't use ML--

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:59.820
that we're adding to
the product as we speak.

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:01.650
If I think longer-term,
there's a lot

00:36:01.650 --> 00:36:04.110
of integrations we have
planned between Stackdriver

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:06.900
Profiler and Stackdriver Trace.

00:36:06.900 --> 00:36:10.084
In particular, this will make
tail latency analysis really,

00:36:10.084 --> 00:36:10.750
really powerful.

00:36:10.750 --> 00:36:15.270
So trying to reduce the
latency in the 95th percentile

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.910
case or 99th percentile case.

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:19.439
And when I think
even longer-term,

00:36:19.439 --> 00:36:21.480
that's when things like
ML come into the picture.

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:24.830
And it's certainly not just
for Profiler, but in general,

00:36:24.830 --> 00:36:27.060
just using ML on all of the
signals that we collect,

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:29.760
whether those be traces,
metrics, profiles, and so on.

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:31.980
And so we will be making
investments in that.

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:34.530
And we have some people
already thinking about this.

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:36.420
But Profiler's in an
early enough state

00:36:36.420 --> 00:36:39.750
that we're mostly focused
on just adding features

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:42.180
that developers can
use on their own

00:36:42.180 --> 00:36:46.080
rather than having computers
solve it for them to identify

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:48.270
places of poor performance
or identify areas

00:36:48.270 --> 00:36:52.570
where they can improve the
speed of their application.

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:55.110
We have some other similar
really clever features

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:57.100
coming to Stackdriver Trace.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.340
So as I mentioned
during the presentation,

00:36:59.340 --> 00:37:02.260
we do random sampling
for requests today.

00:37:02.260 --> 00:37:04.695
And so this is useful, because
it gives you a good profile

00:37:04.695 --> 00:37:05.820
of the latency of your app.

00:37:05.820 --> 00:37:07.630
But one of the challenges
is that, of course,

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:09.171
most of the requests
that you capture

00:37:09.171 --> 00:37:10.680
might not be interesting.

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:12.360
We're going to switch
that pretty soon

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:14.200
to what we're calling
deferred sampling,

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:16.830
so we can sample based
on accumulative latency

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:19.319
or the presence of
errors, which will also

00:37:19.319 --> 00:37:20.610
improve the sampling for trace.

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:23.400
Much like I mentioned we're
investing in smart profiling

00:37:23.400 --> 00:37:25.440
features, we're making
these investments

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:27.020
to make tracing
much more powerful

00:37:27.020 --> 00:37:29.670
and effectively just a smarter
distributed tracing system.

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:31.350
And those features are being
added to the OpenCensus

00:37:31.350 --> 00:37:32.933
project, so that not
just Stackdriver,

00:37:32.933 --> 00:37:36.180
but other vendors can
benefit from them as well.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:37.090
Next question.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:38.430
I don't use Stackdriver today.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:40.225
How hard is it to
get started with APM?

00:37:40.225 --> 00:37:41.850
How long does it take
for my developers

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:44.230
to go from zero
to ready to debug?

00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:45.660
That's a good question.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:47.700
So Stackdriver is
really, really easy

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:51.390
to get started with,
particularly Stackdriver APM.

00:37:51.390 --> 00:37:55.170
So today-- I can
actually speak to this

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.630
very directly, as I instrumented
the Sock Shop service

00:37:57.630 --> 00:37:59.610
that you saw during the demo.

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:01.620
If you're starting from
scratch, so you've never

00:38:01.620 --> 00:38:06.030
used any kind of APM or tracing
or profiling system before,

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:08.190
what you do is you get
the OpenCensus libraries.

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:09.000
You link those to your app.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:10.708
You get the one for
the correct language.

00:38:10.708 --> 00:38:12.540
In most languages, that
will mean that once

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:14.373
you do that, you'll get
traces and app-level

00:38:14.373 --> 00:38:15.810
metrics automatically.

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:17.880
In some languages,
particularly Java,

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:19.680
you have to do a small
amount of work just

00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.630
to plumb through
the correlation.

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:23.425
We have a lot of hooks
that make it easy.

00:38:23.425 --> 00:38:25.050
But we can't do it
fully automatically,

00:38:25.050 --> 00:38:27.240
particularly if you're
crossing thread boundaries.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:30.030
So you do a bit of work to
get trace correlation working.

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:33.040
Even if you don't do that,
you'll still get the metrics.

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:36.377
Then you can link the
Profiler and Debugger agents

00:38:36.377 --> 00:38:37.210
to your application.

00:38:37.210 --> 00:38:39.150
So in Java, that's a
Java agent that you put

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:40.140
in your Java [INAUDIBLE] path.

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:41.556
In other languages,
it's typically

00:38:41.556 --> 00:38:43.920
just a library you link
to your code and redeploy.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.240
If you're running
on GCP, for example,

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:48.540
if you're running on GKE, as
with this demo, that's all

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:49.170
you need to do.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:51.420
You don't need to supply
credentials or anything else.

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:53.190
We pick those up automatically.

00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:55.590
And so with a few
minutes of work,

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:58.500
you can get most of
these tools or if not all

00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:00.940
of these tools
working perfectly.

00:39:00.940 --> 00:39:02.740
If you're running
on-premises, you'll

00:39:02.740 --> 00:39:06.070
need to supply your service
account credentials, which

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.080
I think you can do via
environment variables

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:12.310
or just via you can embed
them directly in your code.

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:14.890
You can do that, and then
your APM instrumentation

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:16.360
will start responding
back to us.

00:39:20.182 --> 00:39:21.640
The second part of
the question was

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:23.139
how long it'll take
developers to go

00:39:23.139 --> 00:39:24.430
from zero to ready to debug.

00:39:24.430 --> 00:39:26.555
These tools are very intuitive.

00:39:26.555 --> 00:39:28.930
If you think of the UI that
we have for Stackdriver Trace

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.750
or Stackdriver Debugger, this--

00:39:31.750 --> 00:39:35.476
Debugger, in particular, we
try and emulate a classic IDE

00:39:35.476 --> 00:39:36.600
interface that you've used.

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:38.530
If you use really
any text editor,

00:39:38.530 --> 00:39:40.450
but in particular,
IntelliJ or Visual Studio,

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:43.160
which have built-in debugging,
we try and emulate that.

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:45.160
We found that for Debugger,
like, engineers just

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:47.224
get it almost instantly.

00:39:47.224 --> 00:39:48.640
It's exactly what
they're used to.

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:50.470
It works exactly as
you would expect.

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:53.740
And there's no need to even
attach to particular instances.

00:39:53.740 --> 00:39:56.710
So we found that teams
are able to get started

00:39:56.710 --> 00:39:57.912
with this really quickly.

00:39:57.912 --> 00:39:59.620
We're doing a lot of
work later this year

00:39:59.620 --> 00:40:02.170
to better integrate
these products, and so

00:40:02.170 --> 00:40:03.970
to provide a single
seamless UI, where

00:40:03.970 --> 00:40:07.824
you can jump between traces,
errors, snapshots, and so on.

00:40:07.824 --> 00:40:10.240
That should make it even easier
for people to get started.

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:12.310
But in its current
implementation,

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:14.020
I think Stackdriver
APM is pretty easy

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:15.930
to get started with.

00:40:15.930 --> 00:40:16.450
All right.

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:18.670
So that wraps up our
list of questions.

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:20.390
I think we're all done.

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:22.090
So thank you very
much for watching.

00:40:22.090 --> 00:40:23.180
And I'll see you soon.

00:40:23.180 --> 00:40:24.730
Bye.

